  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0202_6b09c71a6988.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 254px;\" /></p><p>A junction diode is said to be forward-biased when the positive terminal of the external battery is connected to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>-</mo></math>region and the negative terminal to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo></math>region of the diode.</p><p>The potential of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>-</mo></math>side is more positive that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo></math>side, hence diode is in forward biasing. In&nbsp;forward biasing it acts as short&nbsp;circuit, hence&nbsp;current flows.</p><p>In case of forward biased&nbsp;circuit P-junction should be at higher potential.&nbsp;<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0202_1600eabe58f2.jpg\" style=\"width: 311px; height: 63px;\" /></p>"
  }
}